{% extends '_layout.jinja' %}
{% import 'components.jinja' as ui with context %}
{% block title %}The night yard · wireframe{% endblock %}
{% block description %}How the wireframe site is drawn: the night-yard metaphor, the beam colours and their contrast, the typefaces, the wordmark, and where the illustrations came from.{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
{% set metaphor = [
  ('A train of bytes', 'The incoming byte stream', 'Short cyan pulses arriving on one track'),
  ('Uncoupling', 'FrameCodec decoding frames', 'Wagons separated at each coupling'),
  ('A wagon', 'A frame', 'A cyan wireframe box'),
  ('The waybill', 'The envelope: message ID, correlation ID, payload', 'What the yard reads to route a wagon'),
  ('The points', 'The router selecting one route by message ID', 'One lit route; the others stay dark'),
  ('The weighbridge', 'Middleware around the handler', 'A platform every wagon crosses, in and out'),
  ('A siding', 'A handler', 'The end of one route'),
  ('A long load', 'A fragmented message', 'One load on several wagons, rejoined in the shed'),
  ('Express and slow lines', 'High- and low-priority push queues', 'Two lines merging fairly at one junction'),
] %}
{% set beams = [
  ('night', 'Night', '#030e0c', 'The field every page is drawn on', 'Ground'),
  ('surface', 'Surface', '#091a16', 'Panels and code', 'Ground'),
  ('raised', 'Raised', '#102720', 'A panel inside a panel', 'Ground'),
  ('text', 'Text', '#f1fff8', 'Body and interface copy', '19.03:1 on night'),
  ('muted', 'Muted text', '#b8d5ca', 'Secondary descriptions', '12.51:1 on night'),
  ('mint', 'Vector mint', '#7cffb3', 'Identity, the route in use, the primary action', '15.68:1 on night'),
  ('cyan', 'Cyan', '#00ffff', 'Frames and decoded data', '15.62:1 on night'),
  ('amber', 'Amber', '#ffb347', 'Traffic on the move; warnings', '11.00:1 on night'),
  ('magenta', 'Magenta', '#ff6ad5', 'The one selected or correlated object', '7.72:1 on night'),
  ('error', 'Error', '#ff687a', 'Errors only, never decoration', '7.01:1 on night'),
] %}
{{ ui.page_head([], 'The night yard', 'How this site is drawn, and why a frame router is a railway yard at night.') }}
<div class="wf-shell wf-landing wf-yard">
  <section aria-labelledby="metaphor">
    <h2 id="metaphor">Frames are freight</h2>
    <p class="wf-landing__lede">df12Every df12 tool comes with a world. wireframe&rsquo;s isrsquo;s tools come with worlds. wireframeEvery df12 tool comes with a world. wireframe&rsquo;s isrsquo;s is a marshalling yard drawn in the beams of a vector display: trains arrive, wagons are uncoupled and weighed, and each one is sent down exactly one siding. The metaphor is held to the library&rsquo;s real structure, and drops away wherever it would mislead.</p>
    <div class="wf-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="The yard and what it stands for"><table class="wf-yard__table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">In the yard</th><th scope="col">In wireframe</th><th scope="col">On the plates</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{% for yard, lib, drawn in metaphor %}<tr><td>{{ yard }}</td><td>{{ lib }}</td><td>{{ drawn }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>A routed message selects one handler. No plate shows one wagon dividing between sidings, because wireframe does not broadcast.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="beams">
    <h2 id="beams">The beam system</h2>
    <p>Flat colour is authoritative; glow is optional emphasis. Each colour has one job, and hue is never the only cue: every coloured mark also has a shape, a line style, or a label. The contrast figures are for the flat colour as text on the night field.</p>
    <ul class="wf-swatches">{% for key, name, hex, role, contrast in beams %}<li class="wf-swatch"><div class="wf-swatch__chip wf-swatch__chip--{{ key }}"></div><div class="wf-swatch__body"><strong>{{ name }}</strong><code>{{ hex }}</code><span>{{ role }}</span><span>{{ contrast }}</span></div></li>{% endfor %}</ul>
    <p>Under <code>prefers-contrast: more</code> every halo is removed and the flat cores remain. Under <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> the hero&rsquo;s beam does not trace and the journey rail is lit from the start.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="type">
    <h2 id="type">Type and marks</h2>
    <p><strong>Sintony</strong> sets every word of interface and prose, at its two distributed weights. <strong>Monaspace Krypton</strong> sets code and byte sequences, with ligatures off so that <code>-&gt;</code>, <code>=&gt;</code>, and <code>::</code> stay distinct. Both are SIL Open Font License faces, served from this site.</p>
    <p>The wordmark is drawn, not typed. Each lowercase glyph is a centreline on a small grid; the detailed mark draws its hollow contour by masking a narrow stroke out of a wide one, then lays the faint skeleton and its vertex nodes inside. The header uses a flat single-stroke version, and the favicon keeps only the rhombus and its axes. All of them are generated as SVG by <code>scripts/generate_wireframe_marks.py</code>.</p>
    <div class="wf-yard__marks">
      <img src="/wireframe/assets/logos/wordmark-1400.webp" alt="The wireframe wordmark" width="1400" height="357">
      <img src="/wireframe/assets/logos/symbol-192.webp" alt="The wireframe symbol, a faceted rhombus" width="192" height="192">
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="plates">
    <h2 id="plates">The plates</h2>
    <p>The illustrations were generated with gpt-image-2 on 31 October 2026 and checked by eye. Every prompt asked for the same thing: isometric geometry, faint underdrawing, crisp beam centrelines with a small bloom, one lit route, and no text, numbers, people, or creatures. Anything that has to be read is in the HTML beside a plate, never in it.</p>
    <div class="wf-yard__plates">
      {% for key in ['two-lines', 'long-load', 'outbound', 'proving-loop'] %}{{ ui.plate(key, sizes='(width < 768px) 100vw, 50vw') }}{% endfor %}
    </div>
    <p>Legal pages carry no plates and no glow.</p>
  </section>
</div>
{% endblock %}
